@page "/how-to-play"

@*
    Was a bare h1 and nothing else, and unreachable besides: the only links to it sat in the branch
    of NavMenu that stopped being rendered once MainLayout got its own nav.

    Written for the viewer rather than the streamer. What a streamer needs is on /download; this is
    for somebody who has just seen the game on a stream and wants to know what to type. Every
    command below was checked against the bot's own commands.json rather than remembered, including
    the aliases.
*@

<div class="top-section">
    <div class="hero-text">
        <h1>How to play</h1>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="white-section-break"></div>

<div class="page-content">
    <div class="section">
        <p class="rf-lead">
            You do not install anything and you do not need an account. If a streamer is running
            Ravenfall, you play it by typing in their chat.
        </p>

        <div class="rf-prose">
            <ol class="rf-steps">
                <li>
                    <b>Find a stream running it.</b> The <a href="/streams">streams page</a> lists
                    everyone playing right now.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Type <span class="rf-cmd">!join</span> in their chat.</b> That creates your
                    character and puts it in the world. Nothing else is needed.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Pick something to train</b> with <span class="rf-cmd">!train</span>. Your
                    character keeps at it whether or not you are still watching.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h1>The commands worth knowing</h1>
        <div class="rf-prose">
            <dl class="rf-facts">
                <dt><span class="rf-cmd">!join</span></dt>
                <dd>Puts your character into the stream you are watching.</dd>

                <dt><span class="rf-cmd">!train</span></dt>
                <dd>
                    Start training a skill, for example <span class="rf-cmd">!train mining</span>.
                    <span class="rf-cmd">!train all</span> trains your combat skills together.
                    Also answers to <span class="rf-cmd">!task</span>.
                </dd>

                <dt><span class="rf-cmd">!stats</span></dt>
                <dd>Your levels, and <span class="rf-cmd">!training</span> says what you are on now.</dd>

                <dt><span class="rf-cmd">!raid</span> and <span class="rf-cmd">!dungeon</span></dt>
                <dd>
                    Join whatever the rest of the stream is fighting. These are the moments a stream
                    plays together.
                </dd>

                <dt><span class="rf-cmd">!village</span></dt>
                <dd>
                    Claim a plot in the streamer's town, which boosts one skill for everyone playing
                    there. How much depends on your level in that skill. Also
                    <span class="rf-cmd">!town</span>.
                </dd>

                <dt><span class="rf-cmd">!clan</span></dt>
                <dd>Join a clan, which shares clan skills and a level everyone contributes to.</dd>

                <dt><span class="rf-cmd">!use</span></dt>
                <dd>
                    Eat or drink something from your inventory. Also
                    <span class="rf-cmd">!eat</span>, <span class="rf-cmd">!drink</span> and
                    <span class="rf-cmd">!consume</span>.
                </dd>
            </dl>

            <p class="rf-meta">
                There are seventy odd commands in total and the
                <a href="/commands">commands page</a> lists them. A streamer can rename or disable
                some, so if one does nothing, ask in chat.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h1>Things that surprise people</h1>
        <div class="rf-prose">
            <ul class="rf-tips">
                <li>
                    <b>Your character keeps playing when you close the tab.</b> It stops when the
                    streamer stops running the game, not when you stop watching.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>Your progress follows you between streams.</b> The same character can play on
                    any channel running Ravenfall.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>You can play while the streamer is offline</b> by typing in
                    <span class="rf-cmd">twitch.tv/popout/[channel]/chat</span>, as long as they have
                    left the game running.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>The website knows more than chat can tell you.</b> Sign in and your
                    <a href="/characters">characters page</a> has your skills, your inventory, and
                    which skill you are closest to levelling.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h1>Running it on your own stream</h1>
        <div class="rf-prose">
            <p>
                Playing needs nothing installed. Hosting it does: you run the client, your viewers
                type in your chat, and the official bot connects the two.
            </p>
            <div class="rf-actions">
                <a class="rf-btn rf-btn--primary" href="/download">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-download" aria-hidden="true"></i> Download the game
                </a>
                <a class="rf-btn" href="/tiers">See the Patreon tiers</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
